            {
              "key": "p_8",
              "unstructured": "etal. 1989) one would expect Futuyma D .J. 1986 Evolutionary biology. Sunderland"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_9",
              "unstructured": "most lens eyes to be useless to their bearers without"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_10",
              "unstructured": "advanced neural processing. For a sluggish worm to"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_11",
              "unstructured": "take full advantage of a pair of fish eyes it would need"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_12",
              "unstructured": "a brain with large optic lobes. But that would not be"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_13",
              "unstructured": "enough because the information from the optic lobes"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_14",
              "unstructured": "would need to be integrated in associative centres fed"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_15",
              "unstructured": "to motor centres and then relayed to the muscles of"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_16",
              "unstructured": "an advanced locomotory system. In other words the"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_17",
              "unstructured": "worm would need to become a fish. Additionally the"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_18",
              "unstructured": "eyes and all other advanced features of an animal like"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_19",
              "unstructured": "a fish become useful only after the whole ecological"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_20",
              "unstructured": "environment has evolved to a level where fast visually"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_21",
              "unstructured": "guided locomotion is beneficial. Because eyes cannot evolve on their own our"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_22",
              "unstructured": "calculations do not say how long it actually took for"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_23",
              "unstructured": "eyes to evolve in the various animal groups. However"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_24",
              "unstructured": "the estimate demonstrates that eye evolution would be"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_25",
              "unstructured": "extremely fast if selection for eye geometry and optical"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_26",
              "unstructured": "structures imposed the only limit. This implies that"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_27",
              "unstructured": "eyes can be expected to respond very rapidly to"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_28",
              "unstructured": "evolutionary changes in the lifestyle of a species. Such"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_29",
              "unstructured": "potentially rapid evolution suggests that the eye design"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_30",
              "unstructured": "of a species says little about its phylogenetic re"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_31",
              "unstructured": "lationship but much about its need for vision. It"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_32",
              "unstructured": "follows that the many primitive eye designs of recent"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_33",
              "unstructured": "animals may be perfectly adequate and simply reflect"
            },
            {
              "key": "p_34",
              "unstructured": "the animal's present requirements. In this context it is"
